# {{.Name}}

This folder is a [Neptuno](https://github.com/robertoplancarte/neptuno)
project: a development environment for several repos, run with docker
compose (or podman compose), overmind and tmux. Run every command below from
this folder. `neptuno compose` runs whichever compose this machine has.

## Layout

- `repos/<repo>/`: each repo's source, as a git submodule. Code changes go
  here, and each repo has its own git history, branches and guidelines (read
  its own `AGENTS.md` or `CLAUDE.md` before changing it).
- `dockerfiles/<repo>/`: the repo's development Dockerfile and its
  `entrypoint.sh`, which runs setup such as `bundle install` at every start.
- `procfiles/<repo>/Procfile`: the processes overmind runs for the repo.
- `environments/`: env files, later wins: `_shared/base.env`,
  `_shared/<env>.env`, `<repo>/base.env`, `<repo>/<env>.env`, then the
  git-ignored personal overrides `_shared/*.local.env` (every repo) and
  `<repo>/*.local.env`.
  `neptuno env show <repo>` prints the merged result and where each value
  comes from.
- `compose.yml`: the containers.{{if .Services}} Shared services are reachable from the
  repos' containers by name:{{range $i, $s := .Services}}{{if $i}},{{end}} {{$s.Title}} at `{{$s.Address}}`{{end}}.{{end}}
  `neptuno init` here adds a default service the project doesn't have yet.
- `Caddyfile`: the reverse proxy, which serves each repo at
  `http://<repo>.localhost` on this machine.
- `neptuno.yml`: project settings, such as repos that run on the host
  (`run: host`) and the MCP servers each repo serves.
- `.neptuno/`: generated on every `neptuno up`. Don't edit it.

## Running things

- `neptuno status` lists every repo and service with its state, processes,
  branch and commit.
- `neptuno up -d <repo>` starts a repo and everything it depends on.
  Always pass `-d`: without it Neptuno attaches to tmux and doesn't return.
- `neptuno down <repo>` stops a repo. Always name the repo: with no
  arguments it stops the whole project.
- `neptuno restart <repo>` restarts a repo's processes, and
  `neptuno restart <repo>:<process>` one of them.
- `neptuno compose logs --tail 100 <repo>` shows a container's output,
  including its `entrypoint.sh` setup.
- After changing a Dockerfile, run `neptuno compose build <repo>` and then
  `neptuno up -d <repo>`.

## Working inside a repo

Edit a repo's code directly in `repos/<repo>`: Claude Code and other agents
run on this machine, in this folder. The repos run in their containers, with
their own language versions, packages and env, so run their tools there,
not on this machine:

```sh
neptuno exec <repo> -- <command>     # such as: neptuno exec api -- bin/rails test
neptuno exec <repo> "<a && b>"       # one argument runs with sh -c
neptuno exec postgres -- psql -U postgres
```

`neptuno exec` starts the repo's container first if it isn't running, runs
the command in the same subfolder of the container when you run it from a
subfolder of `repos/<repo>`, and exits with the command's status. A repo
with `run: host` in `neptuno.yml` runs on this machine: `neptuno exec` runs
its commands in `repos/<repo>` with its env. Inside a container, other
services are reached by their compose names (`postgres`, `valkey`), not
`localhost`.

## Subagents, one per repo

`.claude/agents/<repo>.md` is a Claude Code subagent for each repo, named
after it. It changes files in its own repo only and runs commands with
`neptuno exec <repo>`. Give work inside one repo to that repo's subagent,
and split work across repos into one task per repo, run in parallel, while
the main session coordinates and checks the results. Neptuno writes a
missing one on `neptuno add`, `neptuno up` and `neptuno mcp sync`, and never
overwrites one you edited.

`.claude/settings.json` lets Claude Code edit files under `repos/` and run
`neptuno exec`, `neptuno status` and the like without asking. It asks before
anything else, such as editing the project's own files.

## MCP servers

Repos declare the MCP servers they serve in `neptuno.yml`, under
`repos.<repo>.mcp`, with the path each answers on. `neptuno mcp` lists them
with the URL that works on this machine (`http://<repo>.localhost/...`) and
the one a repo's app uses to call another repo's server from its container
(`http://<repo>:<port>/...`). `neptuno up` writes the first kind to
`.mcp.json` here, so Claude Code and its subagents have them.
`neptuno mcp serve` offers all of this to MCP clients over stdio:
Neptuno's own tools (status, up, down, exec, logs, handing a task to a
repo's subagent) and every repo's MCP tools.

When `compose.yml` has the opt-in `playwright` service, `.mcp.json` also
gets its `playwright` server: a headless Chromium in that container, which
opens `http://<repo>.localhost` just as a browser on this machine does. Use
it to click through a change and check it works. After a navigation, call
its snapshot tool to read the page.

## Skills

`.claude/skills/` holds step-by-step guides for common jobs here: adding a
repo, debugging a service that won't come up, and handing a task to a
repo's subagent. Each `SKILL.md` starts with a description of when to use it.
